t = {
0: 'The Hebrew words here for writing, scroll and remembrance also come together when God told Moses, "Write this on a scroll as a reminder" (Exodus 17:14). Some hear that early written memorial in the record God keeps of His faithful people.',
3: 'The dove can also call to mind Noah\'s dove, which returned with a freshly plucked olive leaf after the flood, a token of peace and reconciliation. Now the Spirit comes down like a dove as Jesus rises from the water.',
5: 'Some hear Ezekiel 34 in sheep without a shepherd. There God\'s flock was "scattered for lack of a shepherd" because Israel\'s leaders fed themselves instead of the sheep. Read this way, Jesus finds the crowds neglected in the same way and has compassion on them.',
6: 'Some note that Joel uses the verb rend only once for both hearts and garments, but in two senses: garments are torn literally, as a sign of grief, while hearts are torn in a figure. God asks for the inward tearing of the heart.',
8: 'In Hebrew, the promise to restore the people from captivity is veshavti et-shevut, two similar-sounding words side by side: shavti (I will turn back) and shevut (captivity). The same ringing pair comes again and again in the Psalms and the prophets when God restores His people.',
10: "In Luke's Greek, Paradise is paradeisos, the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the garden God planted in Eden, where man first lived with God. The name came to mean the home of the blessed, and Jesus promises the dying man he will be with Him there that very day.",
11: "In John's Greek, one verb (pisteuo) is used twice. Many believed in Jesus when they saw His signs, but Jesus did not believe in them, that is, He did not entrust Himself to them, for He knew them all.",
13: 'In Luke\'s Greek, Agrippa\'s question turns on en oligo, "in a little," rendered "in such a short time." Paul picks up the same phrase and widens it: in little or in great, he wishes to God that Agrippa and all who hear him would become what he is, except for his chains.',
14: "In Paul's Greek, envy (phthonos) and murder (phonos) stand side by side and differ by just one letter. Some see them paired because envy can grow into murder.",
16: 'Paul\'s phrase "the harvest of your righteousness" may echo Hosea 10:12, which begins "Sow for yourselves righteousness" and ends with the Lord sending righteousness like rain. The old Greek translation of Hosea renders that ending as fruits of righteousness, the same Greek words Paul uses here.',
17: "In Paul's Greek, simple and pure are two nouns side by side with the same rhyming ending: haplotetos and hagnotetos. Both describe the devotion to Christ that Paul fears they may be led away from.",
20: 'Golgotha comes from the Aramaic word for skull, and Mark translates it: The Place of the Skull. Why the spot had this name is not certain. One older explanation is that skulls of executed men lay scattered there.',
}
for k,v in t.items(): print(k, len(v))
